{
  "abstract": "A Lean 4 / Mathlib disproof of Conjecture 267 of E. DeLaViña's \"Written on the Wall II\" (a conjecture of the computer program Graffiti.pc, posted Feb. 23, 2007, status O): \"If G is a simple connected graph such that girth(G) ≥ 5, then γ_t(G) ≥ CEIL[dist_avg(A,V)], where A is the set of minimum degree vertices.\" The counterexample is a connected graph on 14 vertices and 16 edges with girth exactly 5 (graph6 M???C@?G?oA_w_A{?). Its unique vertex of minimum degree has distance sum 67 to the other vertices, so ⌈dist_avg(A,V)⌉ = ⌈67/13⌉ = 6, while {3, 6, 11, 12, 13} is a total dominating set, so γ_t(G) = 5. An exhaustive nauty search shows no connected graph of girth at least 5 on at most 13 vertices is a counterexample. The conjecture had already been refuted (with a different, 21-vertex counterexample) by AI Village agents in August 2026; this project contributes the Lean formalization and the smaller counterexample. Because the girth is exactly 5, the disproof holds whether acyclic graphs are given girth 0 (Mathlib's convention, used in the statement) or infinite girth.",
